    "body": "While reviewing #36642 I found something else we missed in JSON-RPC 2.0:\n\nIf we receive a malformed request we would assume that we couldn't parse it's jsonrpc version and send an HTTP error.  This is how the legacy jsonrpc server worked (despite that behavior not ever being part of any ratified jsonrpc spec) and we maintain it as Bitcoin \"legacy\" jsonrpc protocol. However it's possible to form a request that does properly indicate jsonrpc 2.0 but is still malformed. In that case, we should not send an HTTP error: the correct protocol is to respond with `HTTP 200 OK` and include the jsonrpc error message in the response.\n\nThe solution is to catch parse errors and then check if we know the request's jsonrpc version before responding.\n\nWe already correctly handle such malformed requests in batches and this is already tested in `test_batch_requests()` in `interface_rpc.py`\n\nOn master, the functional test in this PR actually crashes the node because the assumption is codified. Note that this crash requires:\n1. RPC Authentication\n2. A debug build\n\nSo this is not an urgent security fix, but a bug that might disrupt testing.\n\n```\nnode0 stderr httprpc.cpp:46\n    UniValue JSONErrorReply(UniValue, const JSONRPCRequest &, HTTPStatusCode &):\n        'jreq.m_json_version != JSONRPCVersion::V2' check failed.\n```\n\n\nIn release builds the node doesn't crash, but it returns HTTP 400 with a JSON-RPC 2.0 error body instead of HTTP 200.\n",
